# Functions below 100% coverage on the ubuntu-latest lane, recorded.
# The SET is the gate and may not grow; the percentages are reported, not gated
# (coverage is measured under -race, where a percentage jitters and a set does not).
# Regenerate with UPDATE_COVERAGE=1 on a green run of this lane.
#
# The `ToS` rows are object.Value's interface entry point for each built-in
# wrapper type. Since #puts and #print dispatch #to_s (MRI's rb_obj_as_string),
# the display path reaches each type's RUBY to_s instead -- which calls the SAME
# renderer, so no output changed: all fourteen probed types print byte-for-byte
# what they printed before. They stay because ToS() is required by the
# interface, and they are recorded rather than tested because a test calling
# them directly would move the percentage and hide the fact that the display
# path no longer does.
#
# Keeping a fast path (dispatch only when #to_s is redefined, as the operator
# opcodes do) would cover them and save the ~112ns/call the dispatch costs. It
# is not done: that cost is not observable at program level -- five runs of
# 200000 puts gave overlapping distributions -- and a second serial-validated
# cache for an unobservable effect is complexity bought with nothing.
# The three defined.go rows are an operator SPECIAL CASE that fourteen new
# operator records made unreachable for Integer/Float/String/Array -- defined?
# now finds the method the ordinary way. It is NOT removed, because it still
# serves the receivers binaryOp computes on WITHOUT a record: Bignum,
# Rational, Complex, Time. Settling that needs a defined? census across every
# such receiver against ruby, which is its own measured wave (issue filed), so
# the arms stay and are recorded rather than deleted on a guess.
#
